librtlsdr: account for every async transfer

The callback resubmitted only COMPLETED transfers. A STALL, OVERFLOW or
ERROR completion, or a resubmission that failed, took that transfer out of
the pool for good without marking the device lost (the error count only
trips on consecutive ERRORs or NO_DEVICE). Once the last one was gone,
rtlsdr_read_async() polled forever, since with BULK_TIMEOUT 0 nothing would
complete again.

Teardown freed transfers libusb still owned. A handle_events error broke
straight to _rtlsdr_free_async_buffers(); the cancel pass left after one
zero-timeout poll when the device was lost, and otherwise judged a transfer
done by its status field, which a pending cancellation has not changed yet;
COMPLETED callbacks kept resubmitting meanwhile. libusb calls a cancelled
transfer back later with LIBUSB_TRANSFER_CANCELLED, and freeing the transfer
or its buffer before then is undefined behaviour.

The reader now counts the transfers libusb owns (atomically, since a
callback can run on any thread that handles events, synchronous control
transfers included). The first batch is reserved whole before the first submission and
each failed or skipped submission gives its slot back, so the count cannot
drop to zero while transfers are still going out; a phase flag marks the
submission. The callback resubmits only while RUNNING; a resubmitted
transfer keeps its slot, and any other outcome gives it back as its last
step. Zero while RUNNING with the submission over means no transfer is left:
the device is marked lost and the reader returns -1.

Every exit (cancel, lost device, handle_events error, failed initial
submission) cancels the transfers and handles events until the count is
zero, with no timeout, since libusb guarantees each callback; each pass
cancels again in case a callback on another thread resubmitted just before
it saw CANCELING; a poll that keeps failing backs off 1 ms instead of
spinning. Transfers and buffers (ordinary or zero-copy) are freed only then.
The first error (a failed submission or event poll) is the return value,
even when the device is lost while the reader drains (its transfers coming
back NO_DEVICE); with no error, -1 when the device was lost. The reader
always ends inactive.

Only the count and the phase flag are atomic. async_status, dev_lost and
xfer_errors stay plain fields as upstream has them, written by
rtlsdr_cancel_async() and by callbacks that may run on another thread; the
callback's new RUNNING check reads async_status the same way, and the
repeated cancel covers a resubmit made on a stale RUNNING.

The count and the flag go through a small shim: C11 <stdatomic.h>, or the
Interlocked functions on MSVC, whose C compiler has no <stdatomic.h>
without /experimental:c11atomics. dsd-neo applies 0002 and this patch to
its vendored Android copy (after 0001) and to the librtlsdr it builds for
its Windows, Linux AppImage and macOS releases (without 0001).

Applies on top of 0002-librtlsdr-check-async-buffer-allocation.patch.

diff --git a/src/librtlsdr.c b/src/librtlsdr.c
index 9b7f44c..5aa1bae 100644
--- a/src/librtlsdr.c
+++ b/src/librtlsdr.c
@@ -39,6 +39,51 @@
 #define LIBUSB_CALL
 #endif
 
+/*
+ * The async reader's transfer count and submission flag are shared with
+ * libusb callbacks on whichever thread handles events, so every access goes
+ * through these. MSVC's C compiler has no <stdatomic.h> without
+ * /experimental:c11atomics, so it uses the Interlocked functions (windows.h
+ * comes in through libusb.h). Every access is sequentially consistent
+ * either way.
+ */
+#if defined(_MSC_VER)
+typedef volatile LONG shared_int;
+
+static int shared_load(shared_int *value)
+{
+	return (int)InterlockedCompareExchange(value, 0, 0);
+}
+
+static void shared_store(shared_int *value, int new_value)
+{
+	InterlockedExchange(value, new_value);
+}
+
+static void shared_add(shared_int *value, int delta)
+{
+	InterlockedExchangeAdd(value, delta);
+}
+#else
+#include <stdatomic.h>
+typedef atomic_int shared_int;
+
+static int shared_load(shared_int *value)
+{
+	return atomic_load(value);
+}
+
+static void shared_store(shared_int *value, int new_value)
+{
+	atomic_store(value, new_value);
+}
+
+static void shared_add(shared_int *value, int delta)
+{
+	atomic_fetch_add(value, delta);
+}
+#endif
+
 /* two raised to the power of n */
 #define TWO_POW(n)		((double)(1ULL<<(n)))
 
